---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitPrice from '@/components/tools/calc2/UnitPrice.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="unit-price-calculator">
  <UnitPrice client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the unit price calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The unit price calculator converts every pack to the same unit (grams, millilitres or items) and
      divides the price by the amount. The pack with the lowest price per unit is the best value.
    </p>
    <pre><code>unit price = price ÷ (amount in a pack × number of packs)
extra cost = unit price ÷ cheapest unit price − 1</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Coffee at $3.99 for 500 g is $7.98 per kg. A 1 kg bag at $6.49 is $6.49 per kg, and a 12 oz (340.2 g)
      bag at $2.50 is $7.35 per kg. The 1 kg bag is the best value; the 500 g pack costs 23.0% more for the
      same amount of coffee.
    </p>

    <h2>Units</h2>
    <p>
      1 oz = 28.35 g, 1 lb = 453.6 g, 1 US fl oz = 29.57 mL and 1 US gallon = 3.785 L. Weight and volume
      cannot be compared with each other without the density of the product.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Shelf labels sometimes round unit prices or use a different unit for each pack. The calculator uses
      the exact figures you enter. A lower unit price only saves money if you use the whole pack.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
